  "verdict": "The e-0048 'stage the eval' plan, made operational: stage-1 = single-size pass@1 rough-vs-fine at n=106 on the SMALL model, escalate iff McNemar is significant at alpha=0.05. (1) FALSE-ESCALATION under a true zero gap is P=0.026~alpha, so the staged plan's expected cost under the null (the common prior given the 0/50 floor) is only 13.8x vs the 203.5x of running the full design outright -- a ~15x compute saving for the realistic no-effect case, which is the entire value of staging. (2) But the screen is MISALIGNED with the capstone: stage-1 detects the single-SIZE main effect, while the headline is the gap x model-SIZE INTERACTION. A PURE main effect (rough helps equally at both sizes, interaction=0) escalates with prob = stage-1 power (~0.84 at a +0.14 gap), spending 34-204x on a DiD that resolves nothing. So a stage-1 GO is NECESSARY-NOT-SUFFICIENT for the interaction. Recommendation: run stage-1 on the SMALL model so it screens delta_small (the headline-relevant arm), set the trigger at McNemar significance, and report the stage-1 result as a single-size main-effect screen -- the interaction claim still requires the multi-size stage-2 spend, which staging only DEFERS (gating it on a cheap 8.5x screen) rather than avoids. CAVEAT: design-stage OC, e-0040 copula/rho=0.5/single seed; pass@1 screen (a tuned-pass@k screen raises both power and stage-1 cost)."
